{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Loading Textures with Blender Python.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Loading Textures with Blender Python.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"loading textures with blender python loading textures with blender python","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Loading Textures with Blender Python.md","fileName":"Loading Textures with Blender Python.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Loading Textures with Blender Python","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-08 09:04\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Automated Blender Texturing and Rendering]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Automated Blender Texturing and Rendering","blenderpythonapi.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Loading Textures with Blender Python.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Loading Textures with Blender Python.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"loading textures with blender python loading textures with blender python","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Loading Textures with Blender Python.md","fileName":"Loading Textures with Blender Python.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Loading Textures with Blender Python","heading":"Loading Textures with Blender Python","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The source's Blender 2.78c workflow loads an image datablock from a file path, creates an image texture that refers to it, and places that texture in a material slot. The material is then assigned to the mesh object that will use it.\n\nEach step creates a distinct relationship: the image supplies pixels, the texture determines how those pixels influence shading, the material groups one or more textures, and the object uses the material. Separating these layers makes it possible to reuse image data or combine color and normal effects within one surface definition.","tags"